<p>Jim Jarmusch—the director of <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MwefGellnhk"><em>Stranger Than Paradise</em></a>, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7OHg4Ayewao"><em>Down by Law</em></a>, and <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FliCoMmW8C0"><em>Dead Man</em></a>—recently stepped into The Criterion Collection closet to share the films that shaped his aesthetic sensibility. In the next three minutes, Jarmusch pays tribute to a box set of Pier Paolo Pasolini’s 1960s films, along with Andrei Tarkovsky’s <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NrMINC5xjMs"><em>Mirror,</em></a> placing it even above <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2024/04/watch-stalker-andrei-tarkovskys-mind-bending-masterpiece-free.html"><em>Stalker</em></a> and <em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=je75FDjcUP4">Andrei Rublev</a>. </em>Along the way, he name checks a number of other classics. And then, with a bag full of DVDs in hand, he concludes: “I love Criterion. I always say the Criterion Channel is my drug of choice.” For anyone interested, you can start watching films on the Criterion Channel’s streaming service by <a href="https://signup.criterionchannel.com/">signing up for a free trial here</a>.</p>
